            "concurrency": "Single-threaded assumption per data_dir for this milestone. File system operations should use atomic writes where possible to prevent corruption during simple sequential usage.",
            "edge_behavior": "The source module exposes MILESTONE_ID as the literal 'M10' and handle(data_dir, request). data_dir is a filesystem path; request is a JSON-compatible dict with an op string. Return a JSON-compatible dict. Invalid input, unknown IDs, cross-project access and unsupported operations raise ValueError. Do not return success-shaped error objects. Persistence must survive another Python process; storage format and internal design are Agent-owned.\n\nSupported operations:\n1. analytics.fetch(project_id, receipt_id, platform_url): Requires a PUBLISHED receipt with a known remote_id. Performs an actual GET to '/metrics?remote_id='+remote_id at the provided platform_url. Returns {id, receipt_id, content_id, revision, channel, fetched_at, metrics:{views, impressions, reactions, comments}}. Rejects negative or malformed metrics values. Rejects responses where the returned remote_id does not match the expected remote_id. Retains each fetched sample separately in persistent storage.\n2. analytics.history(project_id, receipt_id): Returns {items:[samples]} containing all previously fetched analytics samples for the given project and receipt.\n3. analytics.recommend(project_id): Returns {sample_count, confidence:'LOW' when fewer than 5 samples, evidence_refs:[analytics sample IDs], reason_summary, recommendation}. Facts may not be overwritten by recommendations or self-reports. Persist and reuse actual metrics evidence; no invented causal improvement or model score.",
